1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:02,100
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,580
I decided not to mess with these boards for now.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:10,520
I decided to just put together a demo version like this.

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,600
It came out very reliable, by the way.

5
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:17,740
The only thing — I had a small screw-up today.

6
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:19,900
I had to shut down the generator.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,320
Naturally, everything went to hell.

8
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:25,700
These output diodes here got fried, the ones in there.

9
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:28,940
I overcooked them somehow, don't know why.

10
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:30,660
These ones seem fine.

11
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:34,560
Okay, and there's this commotion, but we'll fix it all.

12
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:37,780
In short, a new generator is coming soon.

13
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:40,100
I dressed it up a bit like this.

14
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:43,480
And I put in buttons from my very first generator.

15
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:48,360
I think it was a lucky one, so I decided that…

16
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:51,180
And this whole system, it…

17
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:52,840
I haven't finished it yet for now.

18
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,860
This whole system will be covered with this cover and screwed shut.

19
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:02,340
And, in short, that's what the generator will look like.

20
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:04,480
You can hang it on the wall, no problem.

21
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:07,260
Drill holes in there, I don't know, or whatever.

22
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:10,980
I mean, here's just a 220 V outlet.

23
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:14,680
I was hooking up bulbs here just recently, testing.

24
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:18,700
These ends are sticking out — it's just a lab power supply for me.

25
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:21,700
And the ground comes in here.

26
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:25,800
I mean, that was plenty enough to…

27
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:26,640
So, the power.

28
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:31,300
The power isn't much yet, but basically…

29
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,160
I think I'll reach a kilowatt.

30
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:39,460
The working principle here is the same as the 200-watt one Kapanadze made.

31
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,260
Here it's a bit bigger.

32
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:44,280
So here a nanosecond pulse generator is working, here it is, yeah.

33
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:47,600
Just a slightly different transistor is being used.

34
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:52,440
Bigger than those blockhead transistors that were lying around here somewhere.

35
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:55,080
There it is, I'll show it now.

36
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:58,000
So, I don't use these transistors anymore.

37
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:00,820
Well, because how long can you keep buying them — they blow up.

38
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,760
Well, basically you can't get power out of them anyway.

39
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:10,440
150 volts from this generator is fed to the nanosecond generator.

40
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:11,400
Here's a little ring.

41
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:17,180
Cable, yeah, I mean regular coaxial TV cable wound over the coil.

42
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:20,160
We have the inductor under the coil.

43
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,860
Here we have a removable bifilar wound.

44
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:29,080
Well, that's about it — it's a fairly primitive construction.

45
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:32,380
Well, this is our beloved push-pull, the generator.

46
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,360
There it is, so there are two switches here.

47
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:37,220
These switches are controlled from here.

48
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:40,340
And the nanosecond stage is controlled from here too.

49
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:45,540
The schematic is really simple, dead easy — you see what I mean.

50
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:48,620
I mean the main thing is to understand what to do, and everything will be fine.

51
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:51,180
Okay, I'll show you soon how this works.

52
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:54,580
For now, damn, it's already late.

53
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:57,760
Past four a.m., as they say.

54
00:02:58,400 --> 00:02:59,580
Past four in the morning, to be precise.

55
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:01,120
And I'm still tinkering here.

56
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:04,040
Anyway, I didn't quite manage in time.

57
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:05,900
I think I'll do it later.

58
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:10,120
Okay, good mood to everyone, as they say.

59
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:12,060
Go for it.

60
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:13,480
Big hello to VASMUS.

61
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:14,820
Already doing yours on glass?

62
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:16,340
Very good.

63
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:28,500
By the way, I decided to buy a whole bunch of these glass boxes, so all the haters can't keep saying there are batteries hidden in here somewhere.

64
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:36,700
And this generator I'm about to build — this one I'll just make on plywood.

65
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:38,140
Just everything out in the open.

66
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:41,240
I already started building it, just dropped it for a bit and took up this.

67
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:48,540
And these clear plastic boxes.

68
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:50,960
We'll get into those next.

69
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:53,080
Alright, bye.

70
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:55,840
Alright, see you.
